<p class=cap>Figure 1. Converged Value Function and Optimal Policy Grid Map under baseline parameters (p=0.1, R<sub>step</sub>=&minus;0.04, &gamma;=0.95).</p>
<div style='text-align:center;margin:8pt 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='width:75%;max-width:650px;border:1px solid #999;' /></div>
<p class=cap>Table 2. Quantitative Comparison of Value Iteration and Policy Iteration.</p>
<table class=g><tr><th style='width:30%'>Metric</th><th style='width:24%'>Value Iteration (Alg 1)</th><th style='width:24%'>Policy Iteration (Alg 2)</th><th style='width:22%'>Agreement / Ratio</th></tr><tr><td>Policy Improvement Rounds</td><td>1 (read-off at end)</td><td>4 rounds</td><td>Discovers identical policy</td></tr><tr><td>Evaluation / Iteration Sweeps</td><td>25 sweeps</td><td>185 sweeps (cumulative)</td><td>VI requires 7.4&times; fewer sweeps</td></tr><tr><td>Total State Value Updates</td><td>225 updates (25 &times; 9)</td><td>1665 updates (185 &times; 9)</td><td>VI performs 7.4&times; fewer updates</td></tr><tr><td>Start State Value V(0,0)</td><td>+0.5311</td><td>+0.5311</td><td>Exact match (diff &lt; 10<sup>&minus;6</sup>)</td></tr><tr><td>Optimal Policy Agreement</td><td>100% across all 9 states</td><td>100% across all 9 states</td><td>Identical action in every cell</td></tr></table>
<h2>Observations &amp; Discussion</h2>
<ol><li><b>Why the policy sends the agent the long way round rather than along the row holding the negative terminal:</b><br>Row 1 contains the catastrophic &minus;1.0 trap at (3, 1). With a 10% slip probability in each perpendicular direction, moving east along row 1 or below the obstacle at (2, 0) exposes the agent to an accidental slip directly into the negative terminal. Navigating north through column 0 and across row 2 introduces a buffer of safe cells, keeping the agent safely away from (3, 1). The small extra cost of taking the longer detour (&minus;0.04 per step) is dwarfed by the expected penalty of slipping into the &minus;1.0 trap.</li><li><b>What the value of the start state means in units of reward:</b><br><i>V</i>(0, 0) = +0.5311 represents the <b>expected cumulative discounted return</b> earned from (0, 0) to termination under the optimal policy. An optimal path requires roughly 5 steps: collecting &approx; 5 &times; (&minus;0.04) = &minus;0.20 in step penalties and receiving +1.0 at the goal terminal discounted by &gamma;<sup>5</sup> = (0.95)<sup>5</sup> &approx; 0.7738 (yielding &approx; 0.7738 &minus; 0.20 = 0.5738). Incorporating stochastic slip delays and perpendicular rebounds brings the exact expected discounted return to +0.5311 reward units.</li><li><b>Which method reaches the answer in fewer sweeps and performs fewer value updates in total:</b><br><b>Value Iteration</b> is significantly more computationally efficient overall for this discrete state space, converging in <b>25 sweeps</b> and performing <b>225 updates</b>. In contrast, <b>Policy Iteration</b> requires only <b>4 improvement rounds</b> to settle on the optimal policy, but requires <b>185 evaluation sweeps</b> and <b>1665 state updates</b>. Thus, while Policy Iteration takes fewer policy steps, its exact intermediate evaluations make it 7.4&times; more expensive in total computational work than Value Iteration.</li><li><b>Contribution of the discount factor when every episode ends at a terminal anyway:</b><br>Even when an episode is guaranteed to terminate absorbing at a terminal, &gamma; &lt; 1 serves two fundamental roles: (a) it guarantees contraction mapping under the Bellman operator, ensuring numerical convergence and stability; (b) it mathematically enforces a temporal preference for <b>shortest paths</b>, since delaying goal arrival decays future reward exponentially by &gamma;<sup><i>t</i></sup>, preventing infinite stalling or looping.</li></ol>
<h2>Exercise 1 &mdash; Step Reward &amp; Discount Factor Sweeps</h2>
<p class=cap>Figure 2. Start State Value V(0, 0) and Convergence Sweeps across Step Reward and Discount Factor Sweeps.</p>
