<table class=g><tr><th style='width:8%'>&gamma;</th><th style='width:10%'>V(0,0)</th><th style='width:8%'>Sweeps</th><th style='width:18%'>Row 2 Actions</th><th style='width:12%'>Row 1 Actions</th><th style='width:18%'>Row 0 Actions</th><th style='width:26%'>Agent Behavioral Preference</th></tr><tr><td>0.50</td><td>&minus;0.0617</td><td>9</td><td>['right', 'right', 'right']</td><td>['up', 'up']</td><td>['right', 'right', 'right', 'left']</td><td>Myopic; heavily discounts future reward, taking shortest route.</td></tr><tr><td>0.70</td><td>+0.0768</td><td>12</td><td>['right', 'right', 'right']</td><td>['up', 'up']</td><td>['right', 'right', 'right', 'left']</td><td>Prefers direct route because discounting penalizes long travel.</td></tr><tr><td>0.90</td><td>+0.3541</td><td>19</td><td>['right', 'right', 'right']</td><td>['up', 'up']</td><td>['right', 'right', 'right', 'left']</td><td>Intermediate; transition point between direct route and safe detour.</td></tr><tr><td>0.95</td><td>+0.5311</td><td>25</td><td>['right', 'right', 'right']</td><td>['up', 'up']</td><td>['right', 'left', 'left', 'left']</td><td>High preservation of future return; switches to safe detour.</td></tr><tr><td>0.99</td><td>+0.6974</td><td>38</td><td>['right', 'right', 'right']</td><td>['up', 'up']</td><td>['right', 'left', 'left', 'left']</td><td>Far-sighted; values +1.0 fully, completely avoids trap hazard.</td></tr></table>
<h2>Exercise 2 &mdash; Slip Probability Sweep &amp; Trap Absorption Analysis</h2>
<p class=cap>Figure 3. Start State Value and Trap Absorption Probability vs. Slip Probability p.</p>
<div style='text-align:center;margin:8pt 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='width:70%;max-width:650px;border:1px solid #999;' /></div>
<p class=cap>Table 5. Slip Probability Sweep and Exact Absorption Probability into Negative Terminal.</p>
<table class=g><tr><th style='width:12%'>Slip Prob p</th><th style='width:10%'>V(0,0)</th><th style='width:10%'>Sweeps</th><th style='width:22%'>P(Reach &minus;1 Terminal)</th><th style='width:46%'>Policy Adaptations / Explanation</th></tr><tr><td>0.00</td><td>+0.8062</td><td>23</td><td>0.0000</td><td>Deterministic environment; no slips, perfect safe trajectory to goal.</td></tr><tr><td>0.05</td><td>+0.6729</td><td>24</td><td>0.0042</td><td>Nominal detour path maintained; small noise causes rare accidental slips.</td></tr><tr><td>0.10</td><td>+0.5311</td><td>25</td><td>0.0137</td><td>Baseline noise; failure rate rises as sideways perpendicular slips increase.</td></tr><tr><td>0.20</td><td>+0.2741</td><td>27</td><td>0.0000</td><td>Defensive policy switch at (2, 1) to 'left'; failure drops to 0.</td></tr><tr><td>0.30</td><td>+0.0384</td><td>29</td><td>0.0000</td><td>Extreme noise; agent steers into walls to stabilize, 0% failure.</td></tr></table>
<h3>Explanation: Why the Trap Absorption Probability Does Not Rise Steadily with Slip Probability</h3>
<p>Between <i>p</i> = 0.0 and <i>p</i> = 0.10, the probability of reaching the &minus;1.0 terminal increases steadily from 0.0000 to 0.0137 because the optimal policy remains fixed along the nominal route while stochastic sideways noise increases. However, at <b><i>p</i> = 0.20 and <i>p</i> = 0.30, the absorption probability abruptly drops to exactly 0.0000</b>.</p><p><b>Mechanism</b>: The optimal policy is not static; it dynamically adapts to environmental risk. When <i>p</i> &ge; 0.20, the expected hazard of slipping into (3, 1) while positioned at (2, 1) outweighs the cost of detour. The Bellman update dynamically shifts the policy at (2, 1) from <code>'up'</code> to <code>'left'</code> (steering directly away from the trap) and utilizes wall collisions at the boundary to cancel momentum and absorb slips. This defensive adaptation completely eliminates any reachable path from (0, 0) into (3, 1), driving the exact absorption probability to zero.</p>
